The '''Capuyaquiran League''' was a confederation of island states in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]] that existed from 1755 to 1832. It was established after the collapse of direct Burawi rule over the former [[Badweyn Islands]] and other Capuyaquiran possessions. The League coordinated defence, maritime trade, and diplomacy while leaving its member states substantially self-governing.

The League inherited the royal-blue flag with a white saltire used by the Badweyn Islands. The flag served as a common maritime and diplomatic emblem rather than replacing the flags of individual member states. Its continued use reflected the League's origin in the former Burawi island administration and its claim to represent the archipelago as a whole.

The League's member states varied in language, religion, and political organisation. Its institutions were intended to prevent renewed colonial intervention and suppress piracy, but the confederation did not create a central state. The League was gradually superseded by successor governments, including the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]], and ceased to function as a unified political organisation in 1832.

== History ==
=== Formation ===

The League was established in 1755 during the collapse of Burawi authority in the Capuyaquiran Sea. The [[Burawi Imperial Split]] of 1709 had divided the former empire among the Kingdom of Burawa, the Kingdom of Galwa, and autonomous [[Lacashe]], while leaving the former Badweyn administration dependent on a distant and weakening colonial power. Island governments used the resulting political vacuum to negotiate a common framework for defence and external relations.

The League brought together former Burawi possessions and neighbouring island states. Membership did not erase local sovereignty: each member retained its own rulers, laws, language practices, and internal administration. The confederation instead provided common institutions for matters that individual islands could not easily manage alone, particularly naval defence and the suppression of piracy.

=== Institutions and administration ===

The League was governed through a council of member-state representatives. The council coordinated naval patrols, negotiated treaties, and set common rules for inter-island commerce. It did not possess the extensive taxation or legal authority of a central government. Contributions to League operations were negotiated among member states and commonly took the form of ships, crews, provisions, or customs revenue.

[[Guajicutea]], [[Lacashe]], and [[Asikyira]] were among the principal states associated with the League's regional diplomacy. The organisation also provided a framework for the former Badweyn islands to participate in collective security after the end of direct Burawi rule. Its ports remained connected through older colonial routes, but political authority was exercised locally.

=== Decline and dissolution ===

The League's authority weakened as member governments consolidated their own naval and fiscal institutions. Differences between larger and smaller islands, disputes over customs revenue, and the uneven burden of maritime defence limited the confederation's ability to act collectively. The growth of successor states further reduced the need for a common colonial-era framework.

The League ceased to function as a unified political organisation in 1832, when the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]] was established from much of its former membership. Other former League territories retained separate political identities, including [[Narubi]] and the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]].

== Government ==

The Capuyaquiran League was a confederation rather than a unitary state. Member governments retained authority over domestic administration, landholding, courts, taxation, and local religious institutions. The League council handled only delegated matters, principally external diplomacy, naval coordination, and rules governing inter-island trade.

The absence of a permanent central capital reflected this limited structure. Meetings were held at ports selected by the member governments, while administrative records circulated among the council and local authorities. The use of the former Badweyn flag as the League flag gave the confederation a shared emblem without establishing a single national identity over its members.

== Legacy ==

The Capuyaquiran League is regarded as an early attempt to create a collective political and security system among the island states of the Capuyaquiran Sea. Its institutions influenced the later United Capuyaquiran Republic, while its flag remains associated with the political continuity between the former Badweyn administration and the post-colonial island order.

